## J2EE Architecture

### Multi-Tier Architecture

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────┐
│      Client Tier (Presentation)         │
│  (Web Browser, Desktop, Mobile Apps)    │
└─────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    ↓ HTTP/HTTPS
┌─────────────────────────────────────────┐
│   Web/Application Tier (Business Logic) │
│  (Servlets, JSP, JSF, Web Services)     │
└─────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    ↓ JDBC/RMI
┌─────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  Enterprise Tier (Data Management)      │
│  (EJB, JPA, JTA, Message Service)       │
└─────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    ↓
┌─────────────────────────────────────────┐
│      Data Tier (Database)               │
│  (RDBMS, NoSQL, File Systems)           │
└─────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

### Container Architecture

#### Web Container
- Manages servlets and JSP pages
- Handles HTTP requests/responses
- Manages servlet lifecycle
- Thread pooling and request routing

#### EJB Container
- Manages EJB instances
- Provides transaction management
- Security enforcement
- Resource pooling and management

### Component Model

```
J2EE Application
├── Presentation Layer (Web Tier)
│   ├── HTTP Servlets
│   ├── JSP Pages
│   ├── Web Forms
│   └── Web Components
├── Business Logic Layer (Business Tier)
│   ├── Session EJBs
│   ├── Message-Driven EJBs
│   └── Business Objects
└── Data Access Layer (Data Tier)
    ├── Entity EJBs / JPA
    ├── JDBC
    └── Data Objects
```

