#### Advanced Servlet Concepts

```java
// Accessing servlet context
public class ContextAwareServlet extends HttpServlet {
    
    @Override
    protected void doGet(HttpServletRequest request, 
                        HttpServletResponse response) {
        ServletContext context = getServletContext();
        
        // Get application-wide parameters
        String appName = context.getInitParameter("appName");
        
        // Get/set attributes
        Integer userCount = (Integer) context.getAttribute("userCount");
        context.setAttribute("userCount", userCount + 1);
        
        // Get real path (for file operations)
        String filePath = context.getRealPath("/uploads/");
        
        // Log messages
        context.log("Processing request");
    }
}

// Async servlet processing
@WebServlet(urlPatterns = "/async", asyncSupported = true)
public class AsyncServlet extends HttpServlet {
    
    @Override
    protected void doGet(HttpServletRequest request, 
                        HttpServletResponse response) {
        
        AsyncContext asyncContext = request.startAsync();
        asyncContext.setTimeout(30000); // 30 seconds
        
        asyncContext.start(() -> {
            try {
                // Long-running operation
                doHeavyProcessing();
                
                response.setContentType("text/plain");
                response.getWriter().println("Processing complete");
            } catch (Exception e) {
                asyncContext.complete();
            }
            asyncContext.complete();
        });
    }
}
```

**Keypoints for Servlets:**
- Foundation of J2EE web layer
- Lifecycle: init() → service(doGet/doPost) → destroy()
- One servlet instance handles multiple requests in different threads
- Container manages pooling and resource lifecycle
- Use @WebServlet annotation for modern configuration
- Async servlets for long-running operations
- ServletContext provides application-wide access
- Always set proper content type before sending response

### 2. JSP (JavaServer Pages)

#### What is JSP?

JSP is a server-side technology that allows developers to embed Java code within HTML markup. It simplifies the development of dynamic web pages by:

1. **Separation of Presentation and Logic**: HTML for presentation, Java for logic
2. **Code Reuse**: Share Java objects and methods across pages
3. **Implicit Objects**: Built-in variables for request, response, session, etc.
4. **Custom Tags**: Extend HTML with custom functionality
5. **Expression Language (EL)**: Simplify variable access without scriptlets

#### JSP Compilation Process

```
JSP File (.jsp)
    ↓
JSP Engine translates to Java code
    ↓
Servlet Class (.java)
    ↓
Java Compiler
    ↓
Servlet Class (.class)
    ↓
Servlet loads in container
    ↓
service() method called for requests
```

#### JSP Syntax Elements

```jsp
<!-- 1. Directives (processed at translation time) -->
<%@ page language="java" contentType="text/html" pageEncoding="UTF-8" %>
<%@ page import="java.util.*, java.io.*" %>
<%@ taglib uri="http://java.sun.com/jsp/jstl/core" prefix="c" %>

<!-- 2. Declarations (define class-level variables/methods) -->
<%! 
    private String appName = "MyApp";
    private int counter = 0;
    
    public String getGreeting() {
        return "Hello from JSP";
    }
%>

<!-- 3. Scriptlets (Java code executed for each request) -->
<%
    String username = request.getParameter("username");
    Date currentDate = new Date();
    
    if (username != null && !username.isEmpty()) {
        out.println("Welcome, " + username);
    }
%>

<!-- 4. Expressions (evaluate and output value) -->
<p>Current time: <%= new java.util.Date() %></p>
<p>App Name: <%= appName %></p>

<!-- 5. Comments -->
<!-- This is HTML comment, visible in page source -->
<%-- This is JSP comment, not visible in page source --%>

<!-- 6. JSTL Tags (avoid scriptlets for better code) -->
<c:if test="${username != null}">
    <p>Hello, ${username}!</p>
</c:if>

<c:forEach var="item" items="${list}">
    <p>${item.name} - ${item.price}</p>
</c:forEach>

<c:choose>
    <c:when test="${score >= 80}">Grade: A</c:when>
    <c:when test="${score >= 70}">Grade: B</c:when>
    <c:otherwise>Grade: C</c:otherwise>
</c:choose>
```

#### JSP Page Directive

```jsp
<%@ page 
    language="java"
    extends="com.example.CustomHttpJspPage"
    import="java.util.*,java.io.*,java.sql.*"
    session="true"
    buffer="8kb"
    autoFlush="true"
