#### Request/Response Wrapper

```java
public class RequestWrapper extends HttpServletRequestWrapper {
    
    private String body;
    
    public RequestWrapper(HttpServletRequest request) 
            throws IOException {
        super(request);
        // Buffer the request body for reuse
        body = request.getReader().lines()
            .collect(Collectors.joining(System.lineSeparator()));
    }
    
    @Override
    public BufferedReader getReader() throws IOException {
        return new BufferedReader(new StringReader(body));
    }
    
    @Override
    public ServletInputStream getInputStream() throws IOException {
        return new ServletInputStream() {
            private int index = 0;
            
            @Override
            public int read() throws IOException {
                return index < body.length() 
                    ? body.charAt(index++) 
                    : -1;
            }
            
            @Override
            public boolean isFinished() {
                return index >= body.length();
            }
            
            @Override
            public boolean isReady() {
                return true;
            }
            
            @Override
            public void setReadListener(ReadListener readListener) {
            }
        };
    }
}

@WebFilter(urlPatterns = "/api/*")
public class BodyLoggingFilter implements Filter {
    
    @Override
    public void doFilter(ServletRequest request, 
                        ServletResponse response, 
                        FilterChain chain) 
            throws IOException, ServletException {
        
        HttpServletRequest httpRequest = 
            (HttpServletRequest) request;
        
        RequestWrapper wrapper = new RequestWrapper(httpRequest);
        logger.info("Request body: {}", wrapper.getBody());
        
        chain.doFilter(wrapper, response);
    }
}
```

**Keypoints for Filters:**
- Intercept request/response lifecycle
- Filter chain architecture for multiple filters
- Pre-processing and post-processing capability
- Common uses: logging, authentication, compression, validation
- Can modify request/response or short-circuit chain
- Configure via web.xml or @WebFilter annotation
- Request/Response wrappers allow modification
- Dispatcher types control when filter applies

### 4. Listeners

#### What are Listeners?

Listeners respond to lifecycle events in J2EE applications. They monitor state changes in:
1. Servlet context (application lifecycle)
2. HTTP sessions (user sessions)
3. Servlet requests (individual requests)
4. Session and request attributes

#### Event Listeners Hierarchy

```
EventListener (marker interface)
    ├── ServletContextListener
    ├── HttpSessionListener
    ├── ServletRequestListener
    ├── ServletContextAttributeListener
    ├── HttpSessionAttributeListener
    ├── ServletRequestAttributeListener
    └── HttpSessionActivationListener
```

#### Listener Types and Implementation

```java
// 1. ServletContextListener - Application startup/shutdown
@WebListener
public class AppInitListener implements ServletContextListener {
    
    private static final Logger logger = 
        LoggerFactory.getLogger(AppInitListener.class);
    
    @Override
    public void contextInitialized(ServletContextEvent sce) {
        ServletContext context = sce.getServletContext();
        
        logger.info("Application starting");
        
        // Load application configuration
        String appName = "MyApp";
        context.setAttribute("appName", appName);
        
        // Initialize database connection pool
        DataSource dataSource = initializeDataSource();
        context.setAttribute("dataSource", dataSource);
        
        // Load cache
        Cache cache = new Cache();
        cache.loadFromDatabase();
        context.setAttribute("cache", cache);
        
        logger.info("Application initialized successfully");
    }
    
    @Override
    public void contextDestroyed(ServletContextEvent sce) {
        ServletContext context = sce.getServletContext();
        
        logger.info("Application shutting down");
        
        // Cleanup resources
        Cache cache = (Cache) context.getAttribute("cache");
        if (cache != null) {
            cache.close();
        }
        
        DataSource dataSource = (DataSource) context.getAttribute("dataSource");
        if (dataSource != null) {
            try {
                dataSource.close();
            } catch (Exception e) {
                logger.error("Error closing datasource", e);
            }
        }
        
        logger.info("Application shutdown complete");
    }
}

// 2. HttpSessionListener - Session creation/destruction
@WebListener
public class SessionListener implements HttpSessionListener {
    
    private static final Logger logger = 
        LoggerFactory.getLogger(SessionListener.class);
    
    private AtomicInteger activeSessions = new AtomicInteger(0);
    
    @Override
    public void sessionCreated(HttpSessionEvent se) {
        HttpSession session = se.getSession();
        String sessionId = session.getId();
        
        activeSessions.incrementAndGet();
        logger.info("Session created: {} (Total: {})", 
            sessionId, 
            activeSessions.get()
        );
        
        // Initialize session attributes
        session.setAttribute("createdAt", new Date());
        session.setAttribute("lastActivity", new Date());
    }
    
    @Override
    public void sessionDestroyed(HttpSessionEvent se) {
        HttpSession session = se.getSession();
        
        activeSessions.decrementAndGet();
        logger.info("Session destroyed: {} (Total: {})", 
            session.getId(), 
            activeSessions.get()
        );
        
        // Cleanup session resources
        String username = (String) session.getAttribute("username");
        if (username != null) {
            logger.info("User {} logged out", username);
        }
    }
}

// 3. ServletRequestListener - Request lifecycle
@WebListener
public class RequestListener implements ServletRequestListener {
    
    private static final Logger logger = 
        LoggerFactory.getLogger(RequestListener.class);
    
    @Override
    public void requestInitialized(ServletRequestEvent sre) {
        HttpServletRequest request = 
            (HttpServletRequest) sre.getServletRequest();
        
        String uri = request.getRequestURI();
        String method = request.getMethod();
        
        logger.debug("Request initialized: {} {}", method, uri);
        
        // Store request start time for tracking duration
        request.setAttribute("requestStartTime", System.currentTimeMillis());
    }
    
    @Override
    public void requestDestroyed(ServletRequestEvent sre) {
        HttpServletRequest request = 
            (HttpServletRequest) sre.getServletRequest();
        
        Long startTime = (Long) request.getAttribute("requestStartTime");
        if (startTime != null) {
            long duration = System.currentTimeMillis() - startTime;
            String uri = request.getRequestURI();
            logger.debug("Request destroyed: {} (duration: {}ms)", uri, duration);
        }
    }
}

