## Enterprise JavaBeans (EJB)

### What are EJBs?
- Reusable server-side components
- Encapsulate business logic
- Managed by EJB container (transactions, security, pooling)
- Remote and local access support
- Available since EJB 3.0 with annotation-based configuration

### EJB Types

#### 1. Session Beans

**Theory:**
Session beans represent conversational interactions with clients. They encapsulate business logic and are the primary component for handling business operations. Three types exist based on state management and lifecycle:

**Stateless Session Beans**
- No conversation state with client
- Shared across multiple clients (pooled)
- High performance and scalability
- Ideal for service-oriented operations
- All instances are interchangeable

```java
@Stateless
@Local(CalculatorService.class)
@Remote(CalculatorServiceRemote.class)
public class CalculatorService implements CalculatorServiceLocal {
    
    @PostConstruct
    public void init() {
        System.out.println("Stateless bean initialized");
    }
    
    public int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }
    
    public int multiply(int a, int b) {
        return a * b;
    }
    
    @PreDestroy
    public void cleanup() {
        System.out.println("Stateless bean destroyed");
    }
}

// Usage
@Stateless
public class ClientService {
    
    @Inject
    private CalculatorService calculator;
    
    public void doCalculation() {
        int result = calculator.add(10, 20); // Returns 30
    }
}
```

**Stateful Session Beans**
- Maintains conversational state per client
- Each client gets unique instance
- State persists across method calls within same session
- Passivation/activation when memory limited
- Used for shopping carts, wizards, form flows

```java
@Stateful
public class ShoppingCartBean {
    
    private List<CartItem> items = new ArrayList<>();
    private String customerId;
    private Date createdAt;
    
    @PostConstruct
    public void init() {
        createdAt = new Date();
    }
    
    public void setCustomer(String customerId) {
        this.customerId = customerId;
    }
    
    public void addItem(CartItem item) {
        items.add(item);
        System.out.println("Added: " + item.getProductId() + 
            " (Total: " + items.size() + ")");
    }
    
    public void removeItem(String productId) {
        items.removeIf(item -> 
            item.getProductId().equals(productId)
        );
    }
    
    public List<CartItem> getItems() {
        return new ArrayList<>(items);
    }
    
    public double getTotal() {
        return items.stream()
            .mapToDouble(CartItem::getPrice)
            .sum();
    }
    
    public int getItemCount() {
        return items.size();
    }
    
    @PrePassivate
    public void beforePassivate() {
        System.out.println("Passivating cart for: " + customerId);
    }
    
    @PostActivate
    public void afterActivate() {
        System.out.println("Activated cart for: " + customerId);
    }
    
    @Remove
    @TransactionAttribute(TransactionAttributeType.REQUIRED)
    public void checkout() {
        System.out.println("Processing payment for: " + customerId);
        System.out.println("Total amount: " + getTotal());
        // Process payment
        items.clear();
    }
}

// Usage
@Stateless
public class OrderService {
    
    @Inject
    private ShoppingCartBean cart;
    
    public void createOrder(String customerId, List<Product> products) {
        cart.setCustomer(customerId);
        
        for (Product product : products) {
            CartItem item = new CartItem();
            item.setProductId(product.getId());
            item.setPrice(product.getPrice());
            cart.addItem(item);
        }
        
        double total = cart.getTotal();
        System.out.println("Order total: " + total);
        
        cart.checkout();
    }
