#### Password Security

```java
@Stateless
public class UserSecurityService {
    
    @Inject
    private UserDAO userDAO;
    
    // Hash passwords using bcrypt
    public void createUser(String username, String plainPassword) {
        String hashedPassword = BCrypt.hashpw(plainPassword, BCrypt.gensalt());
        
        User user = new User();
        user.setUsername(username);
        user.setPasswordHash(hashedPassword);
        
        userDAO.create(user);
    }
    
    // Verify password
    public boolean verifyPassword(String username, String plainPassword) {
        User user = userDAO.findByUsername(username);
        
        if (user == null) {
            return false;
        }
        
        return BCrypt.checkpw(plainPassword, user.getPasswordHash());
    }
    
    // Force password change
    public void changePassword(String username, String oldPassword, 
                              String newPassword) {
        if (!verifyPassword(username, oldPassword)) {
            throw new SecurityException("Current password incorrect");
        }
        
        User user = userDAO.findByUsername(username);
        user.setPasswordHash(BCrypt.hashpw(newPassword, BCrypt.gensalt()));
        user.setLastPasswordChange(new Date());
        
        userDAO.update(user);
    }
}
```

**Keypoints for Security:**
- Authentication verifies identity, authorization verifies permissions
- Declarative security via @ServletSecurity, @RolesAllowed annotations
- Programmatic security via request.login(), isUserInRole(), getUserPrincipal()
- Web.xml defines security constraints, login config, and roles
- JAAS for custom authentication mechanisms
- Principal and Role principals in Subject
- HTTPS (CONFIDENTIAL transport guarantee) for sensitive data
- Hash passwords with bcrypt or similar (never plain text)
- Session timeout for automatic logout
- Role-based access control (RBAC) for authorization
- Security checks at servlet, EJB, and method levels

